## Issues & Observations

* Inconsistent Toyota payload structure
  Different packet types (0x51, 0x52) contain varying structures.
  → Resolved by defensive parsing and separating event-level and timeseries data.

* Missing vehicle identifier (Toyota)
  No explicit vehicle ID provided in sample data.
  → Used a placeholder (`toyota_vehicle`) and documented as a limitation.

* Non-uniform timestamp formats
  Multiple formats across sources (string, nested JSON, Excel).
  → Normalized using a unified datetime conversion approach.

* Missing and malformed values
  Presence of nulls, placeholder strings (e.g., "-"), and non-numeric fields.
  → Applied safe type conversion with fallback to null.

* Duplicate data (rerun scenario)
  Same dataset appears under different filenames.
  → Handled via deterministic hashing (`record_id`) to ensure idempotent ingestion.

* Dynamic Toyota CAN schema
  Signals appear under different keys with no fixed structure.
  → Flattened into a generic key-value format (`signal_name`, `signal_value`).

* Data quality concerns (GPS & speed)
  Potential invalid coordinates and unrealistic values.
  → Applied validation rules to filter out invalid records.

* Engine session edge cases
  Missing OFF events, duplicate ON events, and unordered timestamps.
  → Addressed via sorting and fallback logic (24-hour session cap).

* Cross-source vehicle matching limitations
  Vehicle identifiers differ across systems (e.g., registration vs TKNO).
  → Matching is assumed based on available identifiers and noted as a limitation.
